# Weekly Digest — 2026-08-28 → 2026-09-03

## Week at a Glance
September's SLT content calendar went from plan to production: six executive slots shipped between 9/1 and 9/3, the week-two slots (9/7 to 9/11) all moved into copy, and the calendar was formally named the team's source of truth for who posts what and when. The August analytics review produced the week's biggest strategic shift, moving the Midwestern company page to culture content only and pushing value posting to the executives' own profiles, on the evidence that page visitors (more senior, smaller companies) look far more like the buyer than page followers do. On the website, the recruiting offerings landing page family picked up a new Retained Search sub-page alongside the in-progress Managed Sourcing and Fractional Recruiting pages. A two-scenario recruiting model graphic (shared talent pool vs. siloed recruiters) was scoped and shipped inside a day for a Captila Partners meeting, and a new mini case study workstream (Victory Beyond the Cup, Jessup, ABS) opened in Linear.

## Client / Work Recaps
- **Marketing team standup + AI sync (9/1):** The executive dashboard is current for the month, with the monthly review defaulting to the last full month. Executives running below their posting cadence move from bi-weekly to weekly check-ins, with lighter and shorter posts encouraged; serious pushback on cadence escalates to Jesse. [[Cole Simmons|Cole]] shared the content calendar as the team's source of truth (5 Midwestern brand posts for the month plus week-by-week SLT assignments with categories and process), and Jesse will self-manage his own posting and claim clips where he is the primary speaker. Status across the team: Cole is moving to [[Matt Johnson|Matt]]'s LinkedIn strategy and the New York footage next; [[Oscar Gonzalez|Oscar]] is finalizing weeks 1 and 2 of the September calendar before moving to weeks 3 and 4 and wants to shift to monthly batch planning; [[Katelyne Young|Katelyne]] is on the Ministry Brands deck (case study visuals pulling from Austin Eidson's portfolio and Dom's film shots) while Brandon continues the scoped sub-offering pages; Jesse is heads-down on the pipeline goal and lead workflows, building retargeting lists from executive, B2B, and paid sources to warm leads for sales handoff, with an Infinite Media ad review coming in the next couple of weeks to cut underperformers and scale winners. The STLT session with [[Austin Daniel]] still needs rescheduling.
- **Graphic for Captila Partners (9/1):** Scoped a client-facing visual explaining Midwestern's two recruiting engagement models, due before a 9am 9/2 meeting. Left side: the full outsourced recruiting function (client collapses into Midwestern's ATS; Midwestern runs intake, pipeline, and offers). Right side: the embedded recruiter (a Midwestern employee working inside the capital partner's playbook on Midwestern's systems). Gloo is the example engagement, and the payoff is a shared, values-aligned talent pool across partners: one search serves several portfolio companies. Locked as a two-scenario flowchart (simplicity vs. complexity) with capital-partner logos on top, Midwestern in the middle, and the talent layer below; explicitly not "RPO" language. First pass by [[Ky]], polish by Jesse; shipped 9/2 as [MAR-1162 — Gloo capital partner talent model visual (siloed vs shared)](https://linear.app/buildmidwestern/issue/MAR-1162).
- **Marketing | Monthly Analytics Review (9/2):** August performance across the LinkedIn company page, Matt's personal LinkedIn, and Instagram. Company page followers grew from 3,099 to 3,164; Wednesday was the strongest day (Thursday led May through July), likely because culture content moved to Wednesdays. Top posts were feel-good employee culture carousels, not case studies or thought leadership, and video underperformed. The August 5 traffic peak (160 page views, 62 unique visitors) lined up with Matt's viral meme post, and visitors skew more senior and smaller-company than followers, which makes visitor demographics the better audience-quality signal. Matt posted 17 times in August against a plan of 10, with reach and engagement decoupling on his biggest posts (copy and CTA to revisit). Instagram engagement stays low relative to LinkedIn, with people posts still the top performers. The first sponsored push ran in the last three days of August through Infinite Media rather than LinkedIn page ads; assessment waits for a full month of paid data.

